_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:215:        repair_fill[color].update(repair_additions)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:251:                    repair_fill[color].discard(q)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:277:            out[pt] = SPECIAL if pt in repair_fill[color] else color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:317:    Repair a one-cell-thick rectilinear line network by restoring hidden crossings, marking red-gap repairs with a dedicated trace color, and then tightly cropping the repaired region.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:327:    - Reconstruct each line color by bridging facing row/column endpoints; a background hole jointly claimed by two orthogonal colors is a hidden crossing and is displayed in the winner's line color, while a bridge created by a red-gap repair is displayed in the fixed special color 5.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/29/code.py:331:    Key insight: Background holes and red-marker repairs are not the same thing. A hidden crossing is inferred because two colors independently need the same empty center, so it resolves back to a line color by fragmentation; a red repair is inferred from marker assignment and remains visibly tagged by the special color, after which the whole repaired network is tightly cropped.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dbff022c/2/code.py:42:   - Palette size is capped at 2-3 pairs so color reuse pressure stays manageable within ARC's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/2a5f8217.py:137:def _gen_pair_objects(shapes, colors):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/2a5f8217.py:139:        {\"pair_id\": pair_id, \"is_placeholder\": is_placeholder, \"shape\": shape, \"color\": color}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/2a5f8217.py:140:        for pair_id, (shape, color) in enumerate(zip(shapes, colors))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/2a5f8217.py:178:    placements = _gen_place_objects(output, _gen_pair_objects(shapes, colors))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/6ea4a07e.py:16:    Invert the single-color 3x3 mask, turning marked cells black and unmarked cells into the marker color's paired color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/6ea4a07e.py:21:    - The foreground color is one of the trained paired colors: teal, green, or grey.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/6ea4a07e.py:24:    - Choose the paired fill color for the foreground: teal -> red, green -> blue, grey -> yellow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/6ea4a07e.py:25:    - Paint every black input cell with that paired color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/6ea4a07e.py:39:    assert foreground in FOREGROUND_TO_FILL, \"foreground color must have a trained paired fill color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/18/code.py:56:#      pair. No invisible property such as numeric color ordering is used.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/18/code.py:831:    built = _build_object_pair(layout, colors, bg, outline_col)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/18/code.py:346:    Repair sparse one-cell-wide colored polylines by turning yellow-controlled endpoints first, then using the updated layout to repair red-marked gaps.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:632:def _assign_pair_colors(layout: list[dict], fill_pool: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:639:    if n_pairs >= n_colors:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:640:        pair_colors = list(fill_pool) + [random.choice(fill_pool) for _ in range(n_pairs - n_colors)]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:642:        pair_colors = list(fill_pool)[:n_pairs]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:643:    random.shuffle(pair_colors)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:644:    pair_color_map = dict(zip(unique_pair_ids, pair_colors))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:645:    colors = [pair_color_map[slot[\"pair_id\"]] for slot in layout]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:789:    colors = _assign_pair_colors(layout, fill_pool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/25/code.py:791:    built = _build_object_pair(layout, colors, bg, outline_col)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/af22c60d.py:53:    Infer the row-reflection and column-reflection constants whose reflected nonblack pairs agree. For each black pixel, collect its row mirror, column mirror, and combined row+column mirror positions that stay in bounds; the known colors in that orbit must be unique, and every black pixel in the orbit is recolored to it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/25/code.py:168:    Repair a one-cell-thick straight-line scene by recoloring singleton markers and fully\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dbff022c/9/code.py:422:    \"\"\"Pick palette pairs, a distractor border color, and the keys used by filled sprites.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/26/code.py:602:    built = _build_object_pair(layout, prototypes, colors_by_shape, bg, outline_col)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/11/code.py:760:    built = _build_local_shared_pair(rng, color_a, color_b, rot_k)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/24/code.py:68:#    Colors are not remapped. Each mirrored pair reuses the same body color on both sides, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/9/code.py:30:#    - `comp_count` 依赖“先把 marker 视作其所属线色再算碎片度”。组合后仍成立, 但所属线色来自全局匹配。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/9/code.py:487:    Repair a sparse rectilinear line network by recoloring singleton markers through a global visible-cell matching, then filling the implied gaps and crossings.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/9/code.py:499:    - At overlaps, yellow rays override other claims; otherwise crossings use the more fragmented pre-repair color, with lower color id breaking ties.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/9/code.py:502:    The closest visible line is not always the right owner. A nearby visible cell can be a shared distractor claimed by another marker, so the true repair only appears after solving the markers together. Detached colors matter here because they contribute valid endpoints even though they never participate in any crossing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/13/code.py:538:    pairs = [(border_colors[0], target_content), (border_colors[1], shooter_content)]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/13/code.py:541:        pairs.append((border_colors[i], int(rng.choice(choices))))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/13/code.py:552:    color_pairs = _sample_colors(rng, num_cups)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/26/code.py:533:    - When multiple repaired colors claim one cell, a yellow tip wins if present; otherwise choose the color whose repaired straight segment through that cell is locally longer.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/26/code.py:536:    The puzzle is not just \"repair markers independently\". Yellow changes the geometry before red repair happens. Removing a marked tail can create the exact facing endpoints that reveal where a red gap should be filled, and the final crossing color must then be read from the post-truncation local segment lengths.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/f45f5ca7.py:79:def _gen_render_pair_f45f5ca7(rows, colors):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/f45f5ca7.py:92:    grid, expected = _gen_render_pair_f45f5ca7(rows, colors)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:10:#      its corner cell `(0,0)` is recolored to a pair color `T`. Each active target corner shows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:22:#    - Only two pairs are used per grid, so the color logic is visible without exploding to too\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:23:#      many unrelated colors. Within a pair, the same pair color repeats on both source and target,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:38:#      The generator guarantees this by using distinct pair colors and exactly one target per pair.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:56:#    - No hidden numeric ordering is used; the pairing depends only on visible color equality plus\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:212:      the target/source shared pair color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:216:      and pair-specific. The source main color tells you what the matched target gains,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:257:    source_by_pair_color = {}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:260:        _canon, main_color, pair_color = source_meta[c_key]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:263:        assert pair_color not in source_by_pair_color, f\"duplicate source pair color {pair_color}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:264:        source_by_pair_color[pair_color] = (c_key, main_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:268:    target_by_pair_color = {}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:282:        pair_color = next(iter(colors))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:283:        assert pair_color not in target_by_pair_color, f\"duplicate target pair color {pair_color}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:284:        target_by_pair_color[pair_color] = c_key\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:285:        targets.append((r, c, kind, pair_color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:289:    assert set(target_by_pair_color) == set(source_by_pair_color), \"target colors must match source accents\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:292:    for pair_color, (src_key, main_color) in source_by_pair_color.items():\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:294:        tgt_r, tgt_c, tgt_kind = target_by_pair_color[pair_color]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:300:            assert v in (bg, 0, main_color, pair_color), (\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:303:            out[rr, cc] = pair_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:309:            assert v in (bg, 0, main_color, pair_color), (\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:310:                f\"paint conflict at ({rr},{cc}) for target {target_by_pair_color[pair_color]}: existing {v}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:317:# --------------- generator (output-first pairwise color-swap replication) ---------------\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:518:def _pick_pair_colors(bg: int):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:529:        _paint_pattern(out, src_r, src_c, spec[\"source\"][1], pattern, spec[\"pair_color\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:540:        inp[src_r + adr, src_c + adc] = spec[\"pair_color\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:545:            inp[tgt_r + rr, tgt_c + cc] = spec[\"pair_color\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:565:    source_colors, pair_colors = _pick_pair_colors(bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:569:    for source_key, target_key, main_color, pair_color in zip(sources, targets, source_colors, pair_colors):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/20/code.py:575:                \"pair_color\": pair_color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/0692e18c.py:31:    - The input is both the coarse placement mask and, by complement, the local\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/33/code.py:69:    - Every connector pair has exactly two boundary cells of a single color from {blue, red, green}.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/33/code.py:233:      and connector pairs themselves are colored red (2), blue (1), or green (3).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:13:#      facing sides match only when those 3 bits are exact complements.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:23:#      (111), so the shape-complement cue disappears. The solver must therefore first isolate the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:24:#      repeated color and only then compare complementary contours.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:40:#      neighbors usually have zero shared-color overlap; complement, not intersection, defines a link.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:58:#      On that color alone, neighboring blocks show complementary notch/tab contours.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:61:#    - Samples therefore force both inferences: identify the common color, then read complementarity.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:75:#    - Correct criterion: shared-color + shape-complement must yield exactly one chain in exactly one orientation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:77:#    - D3 check: all-foreground + shape-complement must yield zero valid chains because the accent\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:109:def _complement(profile: tuple[int, int, int]) -> tuple[int, int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:250:    return trail is not None and lead is not None and tuple(lead) == _complement(tuple(trail))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:299:    - Two consecutive blocks connect when their facing profiles are exact complements.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:413:        lead = _complement(link_profiles[i - 1]) if i > 0 else None\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/f9d67f8b.py:114:    Repair the maroon-stained pattern by replacing each damaged pixel with the color forced by the pattern symmetries.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:24:#      gaps are repaired, each overlap cell is shown as exactly one winning line color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:52:#      marker repair. That fails for hidden half-lines in a tri-color crossing; they terminate one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:328:    Repair a dense one-cell-thick colored line network by recoloring singleton markers, restoring hidden crossing claims, and repainting 2-color and 3-color overlaps with periodic crossing patterns.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:342:    - The repair stage still reconstructs the underlying sparse rectilinear network, but crossings are no longer single-color winners. You must first infer which colors truly meet at each overlap cell, then apply the visible parity/cycle pattern dictated by each crossing's local geometric roles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/10/code.py:3:# Name: 交叉选碎片度更低颜色 + 迭代修复直到收敛\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/10/code.py:403:    Rebuild a one-cell-thick rectilinear line network by recoloring singleton markers, repeatedly filling repairable gaps until stable, and then resolving crossings using the converged completeness of each color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/10/code.py:415:    - When repaired colors overlap, yellow-ray claims still override everything else; otherwise any comp-count-based crossing now shows the less fragmented color measured after the final fill round (fewer connected components), while purely fill-only crossings with no marker bracketing still fall back to the smaller color id.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/10/code.py:417:    Key insight: The singleton markers do not just identify missing cells; some repaired cells create the support needed for later repairs. You must follow that cascade to convergence, then judge each ambiguous crossing by which color ends up more complete after the cascade, not by which one looked most shattered at the start.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/10/code.py:472:    pair = [line_color, cross_color]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/22/code.py:689:    color_pairs = _sample_color_pairs(rng, num_cups)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/2/code.py:150:#    Colors are limited to 2-3 pairs and each input color appears exactly twice: once as a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/2/code.py:189:    - Detect every target marker, pair it with the unique source of the same color, then paint\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/8/code.py:652:    built = _build_object_pair(src_colors, dst_colors, bg, outline_col, drift)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/eval/e9b4f6fc.py:25:        assert replacement != source, \"legend pair colors must differ\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/16/code.py:44:  target frame with its source frame, and within each pair only two colors are in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/16/code.py:271:    - Inside that paired source frame, build a mapping from color to canonical corner pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/16/code.py:344:    frame[\"source_assign\"] = {kind: color_a for kind in pair_a} | {kind: color_b for kind in pair_b}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:3:# Name: 互补色连接 + 有向连接端口\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:27:#     - Realize \"complementary color + directed port\" with four connector colors:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:30:#         complement map: red(2)<->blue(1), teal(8)<->orange(7)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:33:#         2. colors must be the designated complementary pair.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:52:#     - Use exactly two complementary pairs, repeated across samples, instead of an\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:57:#     - The generator rejects samples unless both complementary pairs appear, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:66:#       the fixed complement map.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:116:    return _is_connector_color(a) & _is_connector_color(b) & (a == _complement_grid(b))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:119:def _complement_grid(arr: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:213:    Reassemble the scattered sprites around the fixed yellow anchor by matching directed complementary ports.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:222:    - A legal overlap happens only when one sprite contributes an output port and the other contributes the complementary input color at the same coordinate: red with blue, teal with orange.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:225:    Key insight: An open frontier coordinate now carries two requirements at once: a missing role and a missing complementary color. Seeing an exposed orange input does not mean “any output fits”; it specifically demands a teal output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:332:        assert best_moves is not None, \"there must be at least one feasible directed complementary move\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:357:    assert len(solutions) == 1, \"directed complementary jigsaw must have exactly one valid solution\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/3/code.py:777:    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid directed complementary cbebaa4b input\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/27/code.py:577:    Repair a one-cell-thick colored line network whose singleton markers all share one visible color, using a global endpoint assignment and support-free gap filling.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/27/code.py:589:    - At overlaps, control-ray cells win; otherwise crossings are resolved by pre-repair fragmentation, with lower color id breaking ties only after the geometric claims are established.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:3:# Name: 交叉选碎片度更低颜色 + 输入交叉处显示第三种颜色\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:15:#     output keeps the more fragmented pre-repair color there.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:98:    - At overlaps, yellow-control rays still win; otherwise any explicit or marker-bracketed crossing shows the more complete pre-repair color (fewer connected components), while purely fill-only ties with no marker evidence fall back to the smaller color id.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:100:    Key insight: The extra gray color is not another line. It is a crossing placeholder that tells you where two repaired lines should overlap; once that overlap is restored, the output color at that cell comes from whichever of the two lines already appears less fragmented elsewhere in the input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:933:        crossing_pairs[pt] = colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/21/code.py:986:        forced = {color for color in crossing_pairs[pt] if pt in yellow_rays.get(color, set())}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/24/code.py:24:      - D12/2: src 的可见彩色内容故意拆成多个 4-connected 碎片，再由 8 邻接斜接成一个逻辑形状；\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/24/code.py:30:    